The PS3 target (powerpc64-ps3-elf, ps3toolchain's patch) builds libgcc without the out-of-line
register save and restore routines (_savegpr0_*, _savefpr_*, _savevr_*: libgcc's t-savresfgpr is
not in its configuration), yet the backend may still choose them for a function it optimises for
size, as an ELF target; it then stops with an internal error in rs6000_savres_routine_name, whose
assertion knows that AIX-ABI targets other than Linux and FreeBSD have no such routines (newlib
4.6's vfprintf and vfscanf reached it). The strategy keeps every save in line on such a target.
Applied on top of ps3toolchain's gcc-13.2.0-PS3.patch (OpenRAC, port/tools/ps3/toolchain.sh).

--- a/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000-logue.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/rs6000/rs6000-logue.cc
@@ -382,6 +382,15 @@
       strategy |= SAVE_INLINE_VRS | REST_INLINE_VRS;
     }

+  /* OpenRAC: no out-of-line save and restore routines in libgcc for an AIX-ABI target that is
+     not Linux or FreeBSD (powerpc64-ps3-elf), as rs6000_savres_routine_name asserts.  */
+#if !defined (POWERPC_LINUX) && !defined (POWERPC_FREEBSD)
+  if (DEFAULT_ABI == ABI_AIX || DEFAULT_ABI == ABI_ELFv2)
+    strategy |= (SAVE_INLINE_FPRS | REST_INLINE_FPRS
+		 | SAVE_INLINE_GPRS | REST_INLINE_GPRS
+		 | SAVE_INLINE_VRS | REST_INLINE_VRS);
+#endif
+
   /* Don't bother to try to save things out-of-line if r11 is occupied
      by the static chain.  It would require too much fiddling and the
      static chain is rarely used anyway.  FPRs are saved w.r.t the stack
